                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On March 1, 1948, President Truman in a special message to the Congress,
                    requested an extension of the Reciprocal Trade Act which for the previous 14
                    years had “been an essential element of United States foreign policy.” Secretary
                    Marshall testifying before a House of Representatives Ways and Means
                    subcommittee on May 6, 1948, stated that the act was “the cornerstone and
                    keystone of our foreign economic policy.” For texts, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, March 14, 1948, pages 351–352; and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, May 16, 1948, pages 651–652.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
